Let $n$ be a natural number with $1\le n$. Then there exists a polynomial $p\in\mathbb{Q}[X]$ satisfying all of the following simultaneously:

- its natural degree equals $(n-1)^2$, where $n-1$ is the natural-number subtraction (so $p$ is necessarily non-zero of ordinary degree $(n-1)^2$);
- for every natural number $t$, $p(t)=\operatorname{semiMagicCount}(n,t)$ in $\mathbb{Q}$;
- for every integer $t$, $p((-n)-t)=(-1)^{\,n-1}\,p(t)$, where $(-n)-t$ is $-n$ minus $t$ (lifted to $\mathbb{Q}$) and the exponent $n-1$ is natural subtraction;
- for every integer $k$, if $1\le k$ and $k\le n-1$ (integer subtraction) then $p(-k)=0$.

Here $\operatorname{semiMagicCount}(n,t)$ counts the $n\times n$ arrays over $\operatorname{Fin}(t+1)$ (entries integers $0,\dots,t$) whose every row and column sums to $t$. When $n=1$ the stated degree is $0$, the reciprocity factor is $(-1)^0=1$, and the vanishing condition is vacuously true.
